  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening Life Saving Primary Health Care in Flood-Prone and Affected Provinces in Afghanistan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In line with the priorities of Afghanistan&apos;s HRP (2022) and in partnership with WHO, the de facto Ministry of Public Health and relevant health cluster actors, the project will contribute to the reduction of morbidity and mortality of vulnerable Afghans in the areas of highest needs. Building on existing IOM Migration Health programming and in coordination with the Health Cluster sub-regional teams in target provinces, this project will support 6 Basic Health Centers in underserved areas for provision of primary health care including reproductive and child health care services, Nutrition, COVID-19 vaccination and mental health and psychosocial support services (Activity 1 and 3, AHF 1SA strategy). This project targets a total of 198,000 persons in high-risk flood-prone and post conflict affected provinces (Farah, Helmand and Herat), which are among the 14 provinces with more than 35 percent of the total population in need of humanitarian health services (HRP, 2022). This project aims to ensure lifesaving health care through supporting essential health system functions and emergency response, and preventing, detecting and responding to outbreaks. IOM stands ready to strengthen public health response capacities to save lives, while addressing migration and displacement drivers and mitigating mid-term impacts of the crisis in Afghanistan.</narrative>
  </description>
